Ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI) arises from excessive mechanical stress and strain on lung parenchyma, triggering a cascade of cellular and molecular responses. The primary mechanisms include **barotrauma**, **volutrauma**, **atelectrauma**, and **biotrauma**. Barotrauma results from high airway pressures (>30 cm H2O), leading to alveolar rupture and air leakage into interstitial spaces or the pleural cavity. Volutrauma arises from large tidal volumes (e.g., >10 mL/kg), overdistending alveoli and inducing heterogeneous lung strain, particularly in dependent lung zones due to gravitational forces. Atelectrauma occurs when cyclic opening and closing of collapsed alveoli generates shear forces that damage the alveolar-capillary barrier. **Biotrauma** involves the release of inflammatory mediators (e.g., IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α, HMGB1) from stressed alveolar epithelial cells (AECs), endothelial cells, and infiltrating macrophages, driven by mechanotransduction pathways such as **integrin-FAK signaling**, **stretch-activated ion channels (SACs)**, and **RhoA/ROCK activation**.

At the molecular level, **transmembrane receptors** (e.g., ENaC, Piezo1, TRPV4) and **adhesion molecules** (e.g., integrins αVβ6, α5β1) sense mechanical forces. Piezo1, a mechanosensitive cation channel, conducts Ca2+ influx, activating NFAT and NF-κB pathways, upregulating COX-2 and inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS). Integrin-mediated mechanotransduction activates focal adhesion kinase (FAK) and Src kinases, leading to phosphorylation of p120-catenin and destabilization of adherens junctions (e.g., VE-cadherin), increasing vascular permeability. RhoA/ROCK activation induces actin cytoskeleton remodeling, stress fiber formation, and epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) in AECs, compromising barrier function. Shear stress also triggers the NLRP3 inflammasome, promoting IL-1β maturation via caspase-1 activation.

Therapeutic strategies target these pathways: **low tidal volume ventilation** (6 mL/kg ideal body weight) reduces volutrauma (ARDSNet trial), while **positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP)** stabilizes alveoli and minimizes atelectrauma. Pharmacological interventions include:
1. **FAO pathway activation** (e.g., metformin) to restore metabolic homeostasis in stressed AECs;
2. **Piezo1 inhibitors** (e.g., GsMTx4) or **TRPV4 antagonists** (e.g., GSK2193874) to block Ca2+ influx;
3. **RhoA/ROCK inhibitors** (e.g., fasudil) to preserve adherens junctions;
4. **IL-1β monoclonal antibodies** (e.g., canakinumab) or **NLRP3 inhibitors** (e.g., MCC950) to mitigate systemic inflammation.

**Summary:** VILI results from mechanotransduction-driven inflammation and barrier disruption, with therapeutic strategies focusing on mechanical ventilation strategies and targeted inhibition of Piezo1, integrin-FAK, RhoA/ROCK, and inflammasome pathways.

